{"abstract":"How predators select their prey largely defines ecosystem trophic structure, function and dynamics. In aquatic systems, organism body size is an important trait explaining predator− prey interactions. Here, we used a unique Atlantic cod Gadus morhua stomach content dataset with diet information from over 100 000 individuals collected from the Baltic Sea in 1963−2014, to explore prey size distribution and predator−prey mass ratios in the diet of Eastern Baltic cod. Maximum and average prey sizes increased with predator size, as for cod in other systems. However, the prey size spectra found in Eastern Baltic cod stomachs reflect the low species diversity in the Baltic Sea. In general, Eastern Baltic cod feed on smaller prey in relation to their body size than other cod populations. Due to the truncated prey size distribution in the Baltic Sea, cod cannibalism functions as a compensatory mechanism that allows Baltic cod to reach their prey size potential. On the other hand, small- and intermediate-sized cod prey mainly on a few invertebrate prey species, potentially making them vulnerable to changes in these prey populations. Our results encourage further studies disentangling the relative effects of prey preference and prey availability on cod trophodynamics in species-poor systems such as the Baltic Sea.","author_count":8,"contributors":["Niiranen, Susa","Orio, Alessandro","Bartolino, Valerio","Bergström, Ulf","Kallasvuo, Meri","Neuenfeldt, Stefan","Ustups, Didzis","Casini, Michele"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-332575"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"country":["Latvia","Sweden"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Institute of Food Safety Animal Health and Environment","Stockholm University"],"keyword":["Baltic Sea","Body size","Diet","Gadus morhua"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-DTU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"predator prey body size relationships of cod in a low diversity marine system","src":["dtu"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3354/meps13098","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-332575","identifier":[{"id":"85072721953","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["dtu-5dffb1e2-d0ca-44ce-b99d-b0be5359fa00"],"keyword":["Baltic Sea","Body size","Diet","Gadus morhua","Predator−prey mass ratio","Stomach content data"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-1B7037BC7DD22EA235B1EFAE7D81A97D"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/195076865/m627p201.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3354/meps13098","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2019","yearSub":"2019"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"se","key":"stockholm university","loc":["Sweden","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Stockholm University","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"dtu technical university of denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-DTU","l3nl":"Technical University of Denmark","l3nn":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","namevar":["Technical University of Denmark, National Institute of Aquatic Resources","Technical University of Denmark, National Institute of Aquatic Resources, Section for Oceans and Arctic"],"nora_code":"DI-U-DTU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"lv","key":"institute of food safety animal health and environment","loc":["Latvia","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Institute of Food Safety Animal Health and Environment","used":