{"abstract":"Kepler-20 is a solar-type star (V = 12.5) hosting a compact system of five transiting planets, all packed within the orbital distance of Mercury in our own solar system. A transition from rocky to gaseous planets with a planetary transition radius of ∼1.6 R ⊕ has recently been proposed by several articles in the literature. Kepler-20b (∼ 1.9 R ⊕) has a size beyond this transition radius; however, previous mass measurements were not sufficiently precise to allow definite conclusions to be drawn regarding its composition. We present new mass measurements of three of the planets in the Kepler-20 system that are facilitated by 104 radial velocity measurements from the HARPS-N spectrograph and 30 archival Keck/HIRES observations, as well as an updated photometric analysis of the Kepler data and an asteroseismic analysis of the host star (M ∗= 0.948 ± 0.051 M ⊙and = R ∗ = 0.964 ± 0.018 R ⊙). Kepler-20b is a 1.868 -0.034 +0.006 planet in a 3.7 day period with a mass of 9.70 -1.44 +1.41M ⊕, resulting in a mean density of 8.2 -1.3 +1.5, indicating a rocky composition with an iron-to-silicate ratio consistent with that of the Earth. This makes Kepler-20b the most massive planet with a rocky composition found to date. Furthermore, we report the discovery of an additional non-transiting planet with a minimum mass of 19.96 -3.61 +3.08M ⊕ and an orbital period of ∼34 days in the gap between Kepler-20f (P ∼ 11 days) and Kepler-20d (P ∼ 78 days).","author_count":38,"contributors":["Buchhave, Lars A.","Dressing, Courtney D.","Dumusque, Xavier","Rice, Ken","Vanderburg, Andrew","Mortier, Annelies","Lopez-Morales, Mercedes","Lopez, Eric","Lundkvist, M.","Kjeldsen, Hans","Affer, Laura","Bonomo, Aldo S.","Charbonneau, David","Cameron, Andrew Collier","Cosentino, Rosario","Figueira, Pedro","Fiorenzano, Aldo F. M.","Harutyunyan, Avet","Haywood, Raphaelle D.","Johnson, John Asher","Latham, David W.","Lovis, Christophe","Malavolta, Luca","Mayor, Michel","Micela, Giusi","Molinari, Emilio","Motalebi, Fatemeh","Nascimbeni, Valerio","Pepe, Francesco","Phillips, David F.","Piotto, Giampaolo","Pollacco, Don","Queloz, Didier","Sasselov, Dimitar","Segransan, Damien","Sozzetti, Alessandro","Udry, Stephane","Watson, Chris"],"cuid":["S02-097.75-029298"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["America, North","Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Buchhave, Lars A."],"country":["Germany","Italy","Portugal","Switzerland","United Kingdom","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","California Institute of Technology","Harvard University","Heidelberg University ","National Institute for Astrophysics","Queen's University Belfast","University of Cambridge","University of Edinburgh","University of Geneva","University of Padova","University of Porto","University of St Andrews","University of Warwick"],"keyword":["Asteroseismology","Exoplanets","HARPS N","MASS PLANET","Parameters","Planetary systems","Planets and satellites composition","STARS","STELLAR","Stars individual","Super Earths","Techniques radial velocities","candidates","solar type"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"a 000000000001 000000000009 earth radius rocky planet and the discovery of a non transiting planet in the kepler 000000000020 system","src":["au","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1608.06836.pdf","version":"Pre-print"},"id":"S02-097.75-029298","identifier":[{"id":"000388626700001","type":"wos"},{"id":"85009100612","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-e576b047-1bcd-4c2c-b284-7b5f79daf1ff","ku-a966a5c6-f3cc-4e81-82ed-f4e69b599540"],"keyword":["ASTEROSEISMOLOGY","CA